     "text": "{\"count\": 3, \"rationale\": \"Three disjoint routes reach the target, so at least 3 blocks are needed. (1) h2_1:reach needs only edge0:reach and feeds h4_0:access, so h2_1 or h4_0 must be blocked. (2) h1_2:traverse (from either foothold) leads to h2_0:obtain and then h4_1:traverse, so one of h1_2, h2_0 or h4_1 must be blocked. These two sets share no steps, which already forces 2 blocks. If h4_0 is the block in (1), h4_2:reach is still reachable through h3_0 (via h1_0) and h3_2. Breaking it needs another step outside both sets. If h2_1 is the block in (1), h4_0 is still reachable through h2_2:obtain (via h1_0). Breaking that also needs a third step outside both sets. So 2 blocks are never enough. A valid set of 3 is {h4_0:access, h4_1:traverse, h1_0:traverse}: blocking h1_0 cuts h2_2, h3_0 and h3_2, so h4_2 can never be reached.\"}"
    }
